<h2 id="archetypes">Untapped's Mardu-wedge meta snapshot (context, not the scoring basis)</h2>
<p class="sub">"Mardu" is a shorthand for "white, black, and red, in any combination", a three-color slice of Magic's five colors. The grid below is every deck in that slice currently being played on Arena, pulled live from Untapped.gg over the last 30 days. Mythic-tier winrate is the percentage of games the deck wins when played by mythic-ranked players. Decks in red text have a winrate above 55% with at least 100 sampled matches. <strong>Mardu Discard at 60.1% winrate is the strongest Mardu-color deck in the format right now.</strong> The card ranking above counts how many REAL decks actually run each card, which is a different and stronger question than which archetypes could legally include it.</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Is it stuck at 17 decks?</strong> No. The 17 is not a cap, it is every Mardu-color archetype that cleared a minimum-sample threshold (at least 30 mythic-tier matches) in the live Untapped meta period at build time. Lower that threshold and you get more, lower-sample decks; raise it and you get fewer, more-proven ones. The data was pulled by hand from Untapped, so the page does not yet refresh on its own. A once-a-day auto-rebuild is straightforward to wire up, see the note at the bottom of the page for the three update options.
</div>
<div class="note">
<strong>About this grid.</strong> These are Untapped's tracked Mardu-wedge archetypes for the current meta period. The craft ranking above no longer scores against them: it scores against the real decklists harvested from creator videos, which you can switch between Mardu-only and all-Standard with the Deck pool buttons. Sort them by win rate or by how many wildcards it would take to finish each one. The "most shared crafts" idea lives up in the card table, not here: it is a per-card number from 1 to 17 that says, if you craft that card, how many of these 17 decks it can go into. Each deck below also shows its total wildcards to finish on its detail line.
</div>
